{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/340463-thoughts-on-shadowguiding-and-dark-passions","id":340463,"title":"Thoughts on Shadowguiding and Dark Passions","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-12-14T21:06:17-08:00","id":340463,"title":"Thoughts on Shadowguiding and Dark Passions","content":"Shadow-guiding is a pretty maligned aspect of the game. I consider it a pretty integral part of the Wraith experience, but of all the arguments I've heard against playing the game, it's the one that holds the most weight. The complaint is that Shadow-guiding tends to turn into this vicious circle of fucking over other players, so eventually it just gets discarded. Now, at the very least, you could presume that a mature group wouldn't do this, but even mature people slip up now and again. W20 promises to have some new things to say on the subject, but in the mean time I feel a few tips should be in order.<br /> <br /> The Shadow is a full character in and of itself. It may have fewer stats, but ultimately it has its own ambitions, motivations, and goals. For a good character, those goals should be every bit as complex as they would be for the Psyche. Not every Shadow is going to be cheering for Spectrehood, or for total annihilation. A cowardly Shadow, for instance, might very well be afraid of pain or destruction, or be highly unwilling to join Oblivion's armies. Even for those seeking to become a spectre, being a spectre is more likely a means to an end rather than an end in and of itself. The cowardly Shadow from before might want to become a Doppelganger so they can hide among other Wraiths for safety; that same Shadow would likely not want to become a Shade if it could help it. In the long term, a Shadow's actions should support these goals, and as the Shadow shares a body it behooves them not to be too antagonistic to get what they want.<br /> <br /> ================================================== ================================================== ==========================<br /> <br /> Dark Passions should not simply be the opposite of the Wraith's normal Passions. Ultimately Dark Passions are selfish desires, and because Wraith is built on a basis of Jungian psychology, the Shadow represents aspects of the character's personality he rejects. Now sometimes, it's okay for Passions and Dark Passions to oppose each other - many parents for example both would say they love their children, but may feel anger, jealousy, or resentment at the same time that they reject as &quot;not how they really feel&quot;. In that case, &quot;Protect My Children (Love) 3&quot; and &quot;Sabotage My Children (Jealousy) 2&quot; might be appropriate. However, I would say that I have a Passion for role-playing games, but I don't likely have a counterpoint Dark Passion to destroy all RPGs. That'd be a little silly. Likewise, a Wraith whose Passion is to restore a '69 Mustang probably doesn't have a Dark Passion to destroy it. On the flip side, let's say the wraith of a fundamentalist preacher might have a Dark Passion revolving around his repressed homosexuality; he probably doesn't have a corresponding Passion declaring he must prove his heterosexuality at all times.<br /> <br /> Speaking of repressed homosexuality, it also doesn't have to really be all that evil to qualify as a Dark Passion. The main thing is the character has to feel some sort of sense of shame and distance from it. Now, a lot of Dark Passions will be nasty stuff, but you can wind up with strangely less evil Dark Passions as well. The Wraith of an unrepentant murderer might have a guilt-based Dark Passion that secretly acknowledges his responsibility for his actions. &quot;No it wasn't Them, it was you, asshole&quot; his Shadow whispers, but he ignores the whispers because that personal responsibility isn't a part of his personality he wants to accept.<br /> <br /> Of course, while they might not be &quot;evil&quot;, Dark Passions are rarely expressed in a positive way. Repentant guilt is more likely to be expressed self-destructively rather than in a genuinely healing way, and repressed urges are most likely satisfied in an uncaring, possibly hateful manner. A Wraith who can find a positive way of expressing a Dark Passion may be on the way to resolving it, in my opinion.<br /> <br /> Thoughts? Opinions? Long-Winded Rants?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1136-wolfgar","name":"Wolfgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"646"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T23:30:36-08:00","id":340556,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:courier new\">I agree. <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wolfgar</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Dark Passions should not simply be the opposite of the Wraith's normal Passions.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> My character was a classical Mitty. One of his primary Passions was <b><i>Explore the Underworld (Shame)</i></b>. His Shadow\u2019s primary Dark Passion was <b><i>Be Taken Seriously (Rage)</i></b>. These weren\u2019t opposites, but they interplayed well with one another (and by \u201cwell\u201d I mean \u201ccreated an engaging narrative.\u201d)<br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wolfgar</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Not every Shadow is going to be cheering for Spectrehood, or for total annihilation.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> My character and his Shadow had <span style=\"font-size:11px\"><span style=\"font-family:times new roman\">(\u201chave\u201d if I can wrangle my life-busy troupe together</span></span>) an interesting relationship. His Shadow never lied to him, mentioning (on more than one occasion) that he wants Oblivion as much as the next Spectre; <span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">\u201cI just wanna be the one to flush the toilet.\u201d</span> <br /> <br /> One of the things that helped me (as a player) create my Shadow was <i>Dark Reflections</i>. I concocted my Wraith character, but before I moved onto my Shadow, I tried creating the Spectre version of the same character. It forced me to delve a little deeper into my character\u2019s darker half. <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wolfgar</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> \u2026while they might not be &quot;evil&quot;, Dark Passions are rarely expressed in a positive way</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> One of my favorite NPCs is the character John Soulis from the book<i> Haunts. </i>I think he\u2019s a good example of a character that is *not* a good guy. Or a spectre. His Passions are Lust and Perversion and his Dark Passions are Despair and Hate. The Dark Passion aren't positive, but they're the less vile of the two sets. <br /> <br /> [I think this ended up somewhere between 'thought' and 'long-winded rant.'] </span><br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:8px\"><span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">&lt;signature&gt; something witty &lt;/signature&gt;</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/302-nazfool","name":"Nazfool","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar302_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"753"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-15T01:02:23-08:00","id":340661,"title":"","content":"A fair amount of this advice was in the Shadow Player's Guide. Fantastic book; the essays in the back are golden.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}